## 1. Doubles Hierarchy: `Real > Fake > Stub > Mock` (`U4`, `U5`, `U6`)

Move down the hierarchy only when the real dependency performs I/O, is non-deterministic, or has external side effects:

| Dependency Type | What to Use | Forbidden (`BLOCKER` / `MAJOR`) |
|---|---|---|
| **Subject under test & pure domain collaborators (entities, value objects, mappers, policies)** | **Real** implementation | Mocking or stubbing the subject under test (`U5`) |
| **Application-owned port boundaries (repositories, unit of work, gateways, use cases)** | **Fake** or typed interface mock preserving the port's exact return types and domain errors | Unspecced/loose mocks that accept arbitrary calls or return partial shapes |
| **External HTTP / SDK adapters** | **Transport stub / mock** at the HTTP/SDK boundary | Live network calls (`U4`) |
| **Authorization, ownership & data-scope guards** | **Real** policy/guard code | Mocking a guard to always return `true` / allow (`U6`) |
| **Database queries & schema constraints** | Covered by **integration tests** (`{{verification_link}}`) | Mocking database sessions/drivers inside unit tests (`U6`) |

